MEASURED_AT_UTC=2026-09-08T05:28:36Z
COMMAND=go test ./internal/cli/... -count=1 -timeout 600s -v
EXIT=0
EVIDENCE_EXTRACT=.moai/reports/t571/post-absorb-skiplines.txt (47 lines: every `--- SKIP:`, `--- FAIL:`, `ok`, and `FAIL` line)
RAW_OUTPUT=NOT RETAINED. The full `-v` run was 18,847 lines / 1.1 MB, of which ~99% is `=== RUN` and
`--- PASS` noise carrying no claim this file makes. Committing 1.1 MB of it (plus the same again for
the post-M2 run) would have put 2.2 MB of scrollback into the repository to support two numbers. The
extract above carries every line the count and the name comparison are computed from, so the claims
below stay attributable; what is lost is the passing-test noise, and that loss is stated here rather
than left for a reader to discover. Regenerate the full output with the COMMAND line above.
SKIP_COUNT=30
SKIP_COUNT_EXPR=grep -c '^ *--- SKIP: ' <verbose-output>
SUBTEST_SKIPS=4
FAIL_LINES=0

DELTA_VS_PRE_ABSORB: count 30 = 30; name set IDENTICAL.

The count being unchanged across the absorb is a measurement, not a prediction that it will stay
unchanged. t577 touched the counted package without changing which tests skip; a later absorb may
not be so quiet. The re-pin rule exists for that case, and this file is what it produces.

[HARD] Comparing the names requires stripping the elapsed-time suffix FIRST.
Measured here: a raw `comm -3` over the two name lists reports ONE difference —

    --- SKIP: TestMCPServer_StdioRoundTripSubprocess (0.01s)   [pre-absorb]
    --- SKIP: TestMCPServer_StdioRoundTripSubprocess (0.02s)   [post-absorb]

— the same test, timed differently. A `-v` SKIP line carries its own duration, and durations vary
run to run, so a name comparison that keeps the suffix reports a spurious delta on EVERY run. Left
uncorrected, AC-CHB-006's "enumerate the differing names" clause would hand the reader a permanent
false positive and teach them to ignore it — which is exactly how a real delta gets waved through.

The comparison expression is therefore:

    grep '^ *--- SKIP: ' <verbose-output> | sed 's/^ *//' | sed -E 's/ \([0-9.]+s\)$//' | sort
    comm -3 <pre-list> <post-list>

With the suffix stripped, the two 30-name sets are byte-identical — `comm -3` emits nothing
(verified in this run).

